            <ArticleTitle>False-positive prostate cancer markers in a man with symptomatic urethral Chlamydia trachomatis infection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>501-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0956462412472805</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Symptomatic male urethral Chlamydia trachomatis infection resulted in inflammation of the prostate, with associated increases in both prostate-specific (PSA) and prostate cancer-specific (PCA3) markers with prostate biopsies showing no evidence of malignancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
